'''air-duct''' (ãr′dukt), ''n.'' A duct or passage conveying air; specifically, in ''ichth.'', the communication of the air-bladder with the intestinal canal. It is persistent in physostomous, temporary in physoclistous, fishes.

'''aire<sup>1</sup>†''', ''n.'' An old form of ''aery''<sup>2</sup>.

'''aire<sup>2</sup>''' (ī′re; mod. pron. ãr), ''n.'' [Ir., pl. ''airig''; cf. ''aireach'', a noble, a privileged person.] In ''Irish antiq.'', a freeman; a gentleman; one of the privileged classes. {{smaller|Aires were of two classes: (''a'') the ''flaths'', or those who possessed property in land; and (''b'') the ''bo-aires'', who possessed cows and other chattels. The king was elected by these two classes.}}
{{smaller block|Clansmen who possessed twenty-one cows and upwards were ''airig'' (sing. ''aire''), or, as we should say, had the franchise, and might fulfil the functions of bail, witness, etc.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XIII. 257.}}}}
{{smaller block|The upper classes were all ''aires''. To be eligible to the ''aire'' grade, the freeman should possess, besides a certain amount of wealth in cattle, a prescribed assortment of agricultural implements and household goods.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', IV. 252.}}}}

'''air-endway''' (ãr′end″wā), ''n.'' A roadway or level driven into a coal-seam parallel with a

readings.c2s3:
main level, used chiefly for purposes of ventilation. ''Gresley.'' [Eng.]

'''air-engine''' (ãr′en″jin), ''n.'' A motor employing (''a'') the elastic force of air expanded by heat,
